    <h2>Four Towns, Four Kinds of Remodel</h2>

    <p>The building work splits by geography here in a way it doesn't in a single-character town. North of the Tennessee River, <a href="/locations/florence/">Florence</a>'s older neighborhoods and the Wood Avenue and Walnut Street historic districts drive renovation and period-sensitive remodeling on homes built before the Second World War. South of the river in Colbert County, <a href="/locations/sheffield/">Sheffield</a> brings restoration work — the tall bluff Victorians and the mid-century stock that needs updating without erasing what makes it worth keeping. The newer <a href="/locations/muscle-shoals/">Muscle Shoals</a> subdivisions are additions-and-upgrades country, where families with room in the budget expand and improve rather than move. Tuscumbia's older core rounds out a market where a GC has to be fluent in both old bones and new framing.</p>

    <h2>The Challenges That Come With Old Bones</h2>

    <p>The defining challenge in Shoals remodeling is that the older housing keeps secrets. Open a wall in a 1920s home and you may find knob-and-tube wiring that needs an electrician, galvanized plumbing at the end of its life, framing someone modified in 1975, or rot where water has been getting in quietly for years. That's what makes fixed-price promises on old homes a trap — the honest work is scoping carefully, budgeting a contingency for the unknowns, and telling the homeowner up front that the plaster hides surprises. A contractor who acts shocked by what's behind the wall of a century-old house hasn't done many of them.</p>

    <h2>What Actually Works on a Shoals Remodel</h2>

    <p>The projects that go smoothly share a few habits. Permits get pulled, so the work is inspected and the homeowner isn't left with unpermitted additions that surface at resale. The licensed subs — electrical, plumbing, HVAC — are coordinated and sequenced so the framing, the rough-ins, and the finishes happen in the right order instead of tripping over each other. And the budget carries a real contingency, commonly 10 to 20 percent on an old-home project, so the inevitable surprise is a planned line rather than a crisis. That sequencing and accountability is exactly what separates a general contractor from a crew of individual trades working in parallel and hoping it lines up.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Remodels and Additions Cost Here</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Kitchen remodel</strong> — about $25,000 to $70,000 depending on scope and finishes.</li>
      <li><strong>Bathroom remodel</strong> — roughly $12,000 to $30,000.</li>
      <li><strong>Room addition</strong> — commonly $150 to $300 per square foot.</li>
      <li><strong>Historic restoration</strong> — at the higher end, for the care and matching the older Florence and Sheffield homes require.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Old-home projects carry a contingency for what's behind the walls; new-construction additions in the Muscle Shoals subdivisions are more predictable because there's less hidden history to uncover.</p>

    <h2>Knowing When to Phase the Work</h2>

    <p>Not every remodel should happen all at once. When a budget won't cover everything a home needs, a good GC helps a homeowner phase it — do the systems and the structural work first, the cosmetic finishes later — so money goes to what protects the house before what decorates it. On an old home especially, addressing a failing roof or a wet crawlspace before a kitchen makeover is the sequence that prevents redoing finished work when the neglected problem finally forces the issue.</p>

    <h2>Where the Shoals GC Market Is Wide Open</h2>

    <p>Homeowners planning real projects search "home addition Muscle Shoals," "historic home renovation Florence AL," or "bathroom remodel Sheffield," and they're vetting for competence on a job that costs as much as a car. Most Shoals general contractors run little more than a Facebook page and a phone number, so those high-value searches go to whoever put up a real site — sometimes a Huntsville firm reaching in. A page that speaks fluently about old-home renovation and new-construction additions, town by town, wins the customer who's about to spend serious money, and the <a href="/locations/the-shoals/">Shoals contractor overview</a> shows how uncontested that ground is. The trades a GC coordinates — like <a href="/locations/the-shoals/electrician/">electrical</a> and <a href="/locations/the-shoals/plumber/">plumbing</a> — have the same opening. <a href="/">Sites On Call</a> builds a real <a href="/articles/contractor-website-design/">contractor website</a>, designed for the work rather than dropped into a template. If the big projects are leaving the Shoals, let's keep them here.</p>

